From 39bbefd35f1d78d4418a3b1eb5197f763e71f0e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 22:44:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(parser): record inline markers during block line
 scanning
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Paragraph text was read by two vector scans. Block parsing walked each
line to find its end — and, with tables enabled, to look for a `|` — and
the inline pass then ran the marker classifier over the very same bytes.

The block-level line scan now classifies with the same option-selected
nibble tables the inline scanner uses, appends the marker offsets it
finds to a per-parser buffer, stops at the first line ending, and reports
whether a `|` came before it. A paragraph's top-level inline pass replays
that recording instead of classifying its bytes a second time. `|` is
admitted by one extra low-nibble bit whose high-nibble partner no other
row sets, so the same pass answers the table probe without changing any
other byte's class; `|` is not an inline marker and never enters the
recording.

Recorded positions are candidates only: the filtering that rejects
optional markers which cannot open anything still runs over the block's
own content slice, from the one definition both marker sources share.

Only a paragraph's own content replays. Nested inline contexts, setext
headings, commented paragraphs, and the definition-collection phase keep
the existing on-demand scan, and a test switch runs whole documents
through the original path so differential tests can compare full AST
`Debug` output and rendered HTML.

aarch64 runs a fused NEON scan; other targets drive the walk with the
existing marker scan and probe `|` with `memchr`. Both are held to a
byte-at-a-time oracle for every byte value at every offset, on every
target.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
